<description>
Fill each black 5x5 region's missing interior positions with a fixed 3x3 positional color legend.

Input structure
- The grid uses teal as the dominant background and black for one or more
  5x5 regions.
- Each black region has a black one-pixel border and a 3x3 interior that
  may already contain teal pixels.

Rule
- Overlay the legend 1 7 6 / 4 8 5 / 2 9 3 on the 3x3 interior of every
  5x5 black region.
- Wherever an interior non-center legend position is still black, recolor it
  to the corresponding legend color; leave existing teal pixels, the center,
  and the black border unchanged.

Key insight
- The colored output pixels are not derived from the teal motif shape; they
  are the fixed position labels for the missing cells of each 3x3 interior.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="6a980be1" lines="46-61">
<description>
Project the framed periodic stripe pattern across the canvas and erase the unused frame cells.

Input structure
- A bg canvas with a one-cell-thick rectangular frame of one color.
- Inside the frame are two parallel full-interior marker bars of another color.
- A third color fills the region between the marker bars on periodically spaced rows or columns.

Rule
- Infer the periodic row or column mask from the third-color stripes inside the frame.
- Paint the frame color along those full rows or columns, extended to the grid boundary.
- Extend the marker bars across the full canvas and repaint the source-color stripe cells between them.

Key insight
- The outer frame is not preserved as a border; it supplies the color for the periodic stripes selected by the inner source pattern.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="3bd292e8" lines="13-29">
<description>
Recolor wall-separated background regions alternately, starting with grey in the lower-left region.

Input structure
- The grid has one dominant background color and one foreground wall color.
- The wall pixels form one or more 4-connected one-cell-thick segments.
- Each wall segment separates exactly two 4-connected background regions.
Rule
- Keep all wall pixels unchanged.
- Label the 4-connected background regions and connect two labels when they
  touch opposite sides of the same wall segment.
- Color the region containing the lower-left corner grey, then flip between
  grey and green across every wall adjacency.
Key insight
- The red walls define a bipartite map of regions; the lower-left background
  region fixes which side of the alternating coloring is grey.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="e729b7be" lines="8-20">
<description>
Complete the scene by adding the point-reflected copy of the colored object across the central marker.

Input structure
- The grid has odd dimensions, a dominant background, and a center-row marker color appearing only at the left edge, center, and right edge.
- A single divider color fills the center column except for the center marker.
- All remaining foreground pixels form the object to copy, entirely on the left side of the divider.
Rule
- Keep the input unchanged.
- For every object pixel, paint the pixel rotated 180 degrees around the center marker with the same color.
Key insight
- The center marker is the rotation center; the yellow-looking divider is not mirrored, it only separates the source from the empty target side.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="bd5af378" lines="25-42">
<description>
Rewrite a corner L block into two nested L borders and a diagonal trace.

Input structure
A rectangular grid has exactly two colors: one color forms a one-cell-thick
L on two adjacent outer edges, and the other color fills the remaining
rectangle.

Rule
Normalize the L to the top-left corner. Paint the outer top-left L with the
fill color, the next inner L with the original border color, and the main
diagonal of the remaining inner rectangle with the fill color. All other
cells become color 8, then the original orientation is restored.

Key insight
The corner of the input L determines the orientation; after flipping to a
canonical top-left L, the output is the same nested-layer pattern for every
example.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="54dc2872" lines="52-69">
<description>
Move each marked L-shaped sprite onto the separate pixel matching its marker color.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- Foreground has 4-connected multicolor sprites and isolated one-pixel controls.
- Each sprite has one repeated body color in an L shape plus one embedded marker
  pixel of a different color.

Rule
- If the embedded marker color also appears as a separate isolated control pixel,
  translate the whole sprite so the body L-bend lands on that control pixel.
- Clear each moved sprite from its old position, clear its consumed control pixel,
  then paste all translated sprites; sprites without a matching control stay put.

Key insight
- The marker color pairs a sprite with its destination control, while the L-bend
  gives the orientation-independent anchor cell to place on that destination.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="1ae2feb7" lines="30-45">
<description>
Extend row bars periodically across the opposite side of a separator column.

Input structure:
- bg = background. A near-solid vertical separator divides the grid into two sides.
- One side contains horizontal colored bars; the other side is blank target space.
- Bars may be stacked by row and may sit at different distances from the separator.

Rule:
- Identify the separator as the column whose non-bg cells are one color.
- Treat the denser side as the bar side and collect each contiguous row run.
- Process bars from nearest to farthest from the separator; for each bar, paint its
  color every bar-length cells into the blank side without overwriting earlier paint.

Key insight: Each bar's length is the period for its extension, and nearer bars claim target cells first.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="18447a8d" lines="33-48">
<description>
Move each right-side colored shape into the left slot whose template color is its exact complement.

Input structure:
- The grid has two equal-width slots separated by one background column.
- Non-background rows form horizontal bands separated by all-background rows.
- Each band has a shared template color in the left slot and one colored source shape in the right slot.

Rule:
- For each band, define its destination shape as the cells in the left slot not occupied by the template color.
- Normalize every right-side source shape to right-slot coordinates and match it to the unique equal destination shape.
- Clear the original right-side source pixels and paint the matched left-slot complement with the source color.

Key insight:
The right-side objects are not placed by row order; each one completes the unique left template whose missing cells have the same shape.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="ff2825db" lines="18-36">
<description>
Select the majority interior marker color, then redraw the framed board as that color's frame plus its bounding-box outline.

Input structure:
- The top row is a copied palette made from adjacent equal color pairs.
- Below it is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame around a black interior.
- Non-black, non-frame interior pixels are marker colors; one marker color has
  the unique highest count.

Rule:
- Keep the palette row unchanged.
- Pick the uniquely most frequent interior marker color.
- Clear the lower board to black, recolor its outer frame with the picked color,
  and draw the picked color's interior bounding-box border.

Key insight:
- The noisy marker cloud is not preserved as pixels; only the majority marker
  color and its spatial extent define the output.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="9841fdad" lines="15-39">
<description>
Expand the colored runs from the left reference panel into the blank right panel.

Input structure:
- A uniform rectangular frame surrounds the grid.
- One full-height divider of the frame color splits a left reference panel
  from a blank right panel.
- Both panels have one background margin column on each side of their active
  columns; the left active columns contain two-column endpoint slots, possibly
  connected by same-color horizontal runs.

Rule:
- Keep the original grid and paint only in the right panel's active columns.
- A source run exactly matching the left endpoint slot is copied to the left
  two columns of the right active span.
- A source run exactly matching the right endpoint slot is copied to the right
  two columns of the right active span.
- A source run connecting the endpoint slots is stretched across the entire
  right active span.

Key insight:
The left panel is a compressed legend for the right panel: endpoint markers
stay two pixels wide, while connectors between endpoints expand to fill the
distance between them.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="83b6b474" lines="95-111">
<description>
Translate the colored objects into the most balanced complete rectangular frame.

Input structure
A grid with one dominant background color and several monochromatic
4-connected foreground objects that are straight bars or corner-like line
sprites.

Rule
Preserve every foreground object exactly, allowing translation only, and
place the objects so they cover every pixel of a one-cell-thick rectangular
frame exactly once; the frame interior is the background color.

Key insight
The output shape is not copied from the input: the foreground pixel count
determines possible frame sizes, and the intended output is the valid frame
assembly with the least difference between height and width.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="eval" puzzle_id="f931b4a8" lines="10-27">
<description>
Count the two upper quadrant control regions to size an output made from the lower quadrant pattern.

Input structure:
- The grid is an even square split into four equal quadrants.
- Black is the background.
- The upper-left and upper-right quadrants are control regions.
- The lower-left quadrant is a color source tile; the lower-right quadrant is either a stencil tile or empty.

Rule:
- Output height is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-left quadrant.
- Output width is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-right quadrant.
- If the lower-right stencil has foreground pixels, tile it over the output; each stencil background pixel is filled from the lower-left quadrant cell at the corresponding coarse tile position.
- If the lower-right stencil is empty, tile the lower-left quadrant directly over the output.

Key insight:
- The top quadrants specify only dimensions, while the bottom quadrants specify a two-level repeated pattern: a fine stencil and coarse fill colors.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

